Add [shimforge](https://crates.io/crates/shimforge) as a dev-dependency. It replaces a function at run time for the current test thread, so a test can control `Instant::now` and `std::thread::sleep` without changing production code. The timeout branch then needs one extra mocked read, runs in microseconds, and does not depend on machine speed.

`Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[workspace.dependencies]
shimforge = "0.1.3"
```

`probe-rs/Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[dev-dependencies]
shimforge = { workspace = true }
```

### Example 1: the timeout branch

This goes in the existing `mod test` of `armv7ar.rs`, beside `armv7a_wait_for_core_halted`, with `use shimforge::Session;` and `use shimforge::mock;` added to that module. The clock reports that the timeout has passed, so the loop takes the timeout branch and never sleeps.

```rust
#[test]
fn armv7a_wait_for_core_halted_times_out() {
    let mut probe = MockProbe::new();

    // One poll, and the core is still running.
    let mut dbgdscr = Dbgdscr(0);
    dbgdscr.set_halted(false);
    probe.expected_read(
        Dbgdscr::get_mmio_address_from_base(TEST_BASE_ADDRESS).unwrap(),
        dbgdscr.into(),
    );

    // The clock reports that the timeout has passed, so the loop takes the
    // timeout branch after the first poll, without sleeping.
    let mut shim = Session::new();
    let elapsed = mock!(shim, Instant::elapsed, fn(&Instant) -> Duration);
    elapsed.expect().once().returns(Duration::from_secs(1));

    assert!(matches!(
        wait_for_core_halted(&mut probe, TEST_BASE_ADDRESS, Duration::from_millis(100)),
        Err(ArmError::Timeout)
    ));
}
```

### Example 2: how many times it polls

Mocking `std::thread::sleep` as well pins down the whole loop: three polls, two waits, then the timeout. It still takes microseconds.

```rust
#[test]
fn armv7a_wait_for_core_halted_polls_until_the_timeout_passes() {
    let mut probe = MockProbe::new();

    let mut dbgdscr = Dbgdscr(0);
    dbgdscr.set_halted(false);
    for _ in 0..3 {
        probe.expected_read(
            Dbgdscr::get_mmio_address_from_base(TEST_BASE_ADDRESS).unwrap(),
            dbgdscr.into(),
        );
    }

    let mut shim = Session::new();

    // The 1 ms waits happen, but no real time passes.
    let sleep = mock!(shim, std::thread::sleep, fn(Duration));
    sleep.expect().times(2).returns_default();

    let elapsed = mock!(shim, Instant::elapsed, fn(&Instant) -> Duration);
    elapsed.expect().once().returns(Duration::from_millis(40));
    elapsed.expect().once().returns(Duration::from_millis(80));
    elapsed.expect().once().returns(Duration::from_millis(120));

    assert!(matches!(
        wait_for_core_halted(&mut probe, TEST_BASE_ADDRESS, Duration::from_millis(100)),
        Err(ArmError::Timeout)
    ));
}
```

If a later change polls a different number of times, or stops sleeping between polls, the counts fail the test. `MockProbe`'s own `Drop` check still applies, so a missing poll fails too.

The same two tests can be written for every loop listed above. They complement `MockProbe`.

## Evidence

Both tests above run green on a fork of this repo, on top of `07e777d`: [run on ubuntu-latest and windows-latest](https://github.com/tanglearncode/probe-rs/actions/runs/35488466183), using `cargo nextest run -p probe-rs --lib --locked -E 'test(armv7a_)'`. All 19 armv7ar tests pass, and each of the two new ones takes about 10 ms.
